      "content": "When you use `.copy()` when creating a subset, you're essentially creating a new, independent copy of the original DataFrame. This copy is stored in memory separately from the original DataFrame, so pandas doesn't get confused about which one you're modifying.\n\nHowever, when you use `.loc` to create a subset, pandas doesn't create a new copy of the DataFrame. Instead, it creates a view of the original DataFrame, which is a slice of the original data. This view is essentially a pointer to the original data, so any changes you make to the view will affect the original data.\n\nThink of it like a group of people standing in line for a ride at a theme park. When you use `.copy()`, you're creating a new line of people that's separate from the original line. When you use `.loc`, you're creating a new slice of the original line, so any changes you make to that slice will affect the original line.\n\nTo avoid making copies of everything, you can use the `.copy()` method when creating subsets, but you can also use the `.loc` method with the `.copy()` method to create a copy of the subset. For example:\n\n```python\ndf_subset = df[df['age'] >",
